             

                           CCtest Version 1.2

                              Release Note
 


                              October, 1989

                              By Chenchen Ku



            This release note describes the enhancements of CCtest in 
      this release.  If you haven't run CCtest before, you should refer
      to "CCtest User's Guide" for how to use it.  All the infomation
      in the User's Guide has been updated to this new release.

BUGS FIXED

    1. A bug in CCtest version 1.1 prevents CCtest from running over a
       3C connection is fixed. To run CCtest over a 3C connection, use the
       -v switch to disable all VIO output to the screen.

NEW FEATURES.

    1. Four lights on the CCtest window will tell you the current state
       of the CCtest.

    2. More infomation will be written to logfile when error happened.
 
    3. -P switch will enable a copy of the error information being sent 
       to PRN:.

    4. -z <time in seconds to sleep> switch will put CCtest into sleep for
       the amount of time where you specified in the -z switch (in seconds)
       between copy-and-compares.

    5. Bigger buffer.
       CCtest Version 1.2 has a 5120 bytes of input buffer to store options,
       this feature allows users to write a larger options file.

    6. CCtest 1.2 will abandon the copy-back operation if the first copy-to
       results in an error situation.

    7. CCtest window now will show the mode the program are running on - 
       either OS/2 or DOS.

FREQUENTLY ASKED QUESTIONS & ANSWERS. 

    1. Can I put CCtest in a batch file ?
       Yes, CCtest will return the failure number 1 upon exit, an IF 
       ERRORLEVEL command can then be used to test the return code from
       CCtest and jump to the corresponding error handling routine.  

    2. How many destinations can be displayed on the CCtest window ?
       Only the first 10 desinations will be displayed on the CCtest
       window, even the number of destination is larger than ten.

    3. How do I save the compare-failed files on disk in over-night testing?
       Since CCtest will not save the compare-failed file on disk when it
       is running in Continue mode (-c switch), an alternative is to write
       a batch file to invoke CCtest several times, this way you can run
       each individual CCtest in Break mode and save the compare-failed
       files on disk. In order to keep all the files, remember to use 
       different Machine ID for each CCtest.

    4. The meaning of "destination" is:
       1. A drive name, for example, c:.
       2. A directory, for example, c:\test\cctest.
       3. A network share name, for example, \\saturn\test.
       4. A directory under the network share name, for example, 
          \\saturn\test\copytest\cctest\test1.

    5. Why CCtest was designed to copy and compare files on both directions?
       Try to excercise both the send and receive routines in protocol stack.













 
